>>>>> " " == Mathew W Lippard <mlippard@csugrad.cs.vt.edu> writes:
In article <36f8k3$nmr@csugrad.cs.vt.edu> mlippard@csugrad.cs.vt.edu (Mathew W. Lippard) writes:


 > My Xwindows won't allow root to login, and since my system
 > automatically start Xwindows (xdm) after reboot, root can never
 > login.

 > Does anybody know how to either exit out of xdm or stop it from
 > automatic startup.

 > Please help, I'm useless without my root!

xdm is started up by a file in /sbin/rc3.d.  On my system the file is
named S95xdm.  Scripts in r3.d only get invoked when booting to level
3 (see init(8)).  If you shutdown or boot into single user mode, xdm
will not start.  If you need to be able to become root while in run
level 3, just use su.  To do so, you'll need to add your account as a
member of group system in /etc/group.  


