rdmurray@keene.edu (R. David Murray) writes:

>My biggest question is, why would this noise source affect NFS so strongly,
>yet leave, for example, telnet terminal sessions (across the same segment
>the NFS traffic was traversing) performing apparently normally?

NFS and its users are much more sensitive to network lossage than TCP
because:

1) Users see "NFS server not responding" messages.  [Mostly :-) ]

2) NFS write requests and read replies are usually 8KB long.  On Ethernets,
   this means 6 IP fragments, all sent back-to-back.  Some routers have
   trouble forwarding messages when the target wire has that sort of
   traffic.

   Really ugly things can happen if your noise source could transmit while
   an NFS fragment was on the wire.

3) Loss of any IP fragment forces UDP to retransmit the entire 8KB message.
   In TCP, the data would be separate TCP messages, not IP fragments, so if
   a middle piece of data was lost, that piece would be retransmitted
   while the destination held onto the subsequent pieces.  Even if multiple
   TCP messages were lost, recovery would be more efficient than UDP.

If you have a clean network, of course, NFS over UDP works just fine.
