The following is a summary as requested by some:

>Question: What are the pros and cons of going through the upgrade processes
>(1.3 -> 2.0 -> 2.1 -> 3.0) vs. a new installation of v3.0. I don't have too
>much software on this machine, but I'm also not too comfortable yet with
>the installation process in general, and the mail setup in particular.

My decision: I'm going to do the full install of v3.0, but not until after
I've received my complementary products and layered products CDs.

Before then:
  I'm going to make a more definite separation between my user
  files and my system files. I'm going to make sure I have the proper
  OSF-BASE Pak. I'm going to make sure that I'm up on latest firmware.
  I have already contacted my third-party software vendors to ensure that
  their products will run under v3.0.

Heartfelt thanks to all who responded!

Detailed Responses: (Some nearly duplicate responses omitted.)

 From:  IN%"davidm@cs.arizona.edu"  "David Mosberger-Tang"

  I just upgraded a 3000/600 from v1.3 to v3.0.  After some reports on
  the net that the "upgradeinstall" may not work properly (and my
  general belief that it is almost impossible to make such an upgrade
  work perfectly), I decided to go with a full install.

  This turned out to be a very painless process.  Installing the OS was
  trivial (just follow the instructions).  I did have to recompile some
  programs (emacs 19.27, and gcc), but most old binaries seem to work
  just fine.

  Also, make sure you have the right licenses before installing v3.0.
  In particular, OSF-BASE is essential.

 From:  IN%"mnk@wuerl.WUstl.EDU"  "Menelaos Karamichalis"

  You do not need to upgrade to 2.1.  Just upgrade to 2.0 and then to 3.0.
  After you upgrade to 2.0, you can do the firmware update that supports
  3.0.  The firmware that supports 3.0 does not support 1.3.
  At least the procedure I describe above is what I did with my 3000/500.
  If it sounds like too much hassle, then do a clean installation of 3.0.
  I just did one on my 7000/610 and it was not bad at all(it was a VMS
  system before).  You mentioned that you did not have much software on the
  system.  Keep in mind that this software may not be suppported by 3.0.
  Thus, with the exception of mail setup and other such tasks, you may not
  really be saving yourself time by not doing a complete installation, as
  compared to a sequential upgrade.

 From:  IN%"jeffs@esca.com"

  I have gone through all the upgrades through field test and
  regular releases. The consensus from this group is that you should
  just do a clean 3.0 installation. The upgrades have had various
  problems in the past in getting the right files replaced and getting
  kernels properly built.

  The installation is very straightforward [I generally use the advanced
  option to have control over filesystem placement], and if you run
  'setup' when you are done it is pretty good about stepping you through
  mail and all the other setup procedures required to get you up and
  running.

  Make sure to look at the release notes and ensure that you are up to
  the latest firmware rev.

 From:  IN%"tpb@zk3.dec.com"  "Dr. Tom Blinn"

  If I were in your shoes, I'd probably just back up my user areas and then
do
  an initial install of DEC OSF/1 V3.0.

  If you wait until you get your supplementary "complementary products" CD
(it
  is being shipped this week) and your layered products CD (which should
start
  to ship this week or next), your life will be easier.

 From:  IN%"castor@drizzle.Stanford.EDU"  "Castor Fu"

  I think you'd be much better off doing a fresh install of 3.0 than
  going through successive upgrades.  The drawback is that you'll have
  to  do a dump and restore of your own software, and that you may
  find some things have changed significantly.  The advantage is
  that you won't go sorting through things wondering if they
  had been changed correctly or not.

  The main problems we had going from 1.3 to 2.0 is that the install
process
  saved copies of many files which we had touched, and we ended up spending
  about as much time looking at all the files to see if there were
important
  changes, as we would have doing a fresh installation.

  As we look at moving to v3.0, we will definitely have to do a full
install,
  because we ended up customizing our file system.

 From:  IN%"reeves@zk3.dec.com"  "Jon Reeves"

  First: if you're going to V3.0, you can skip 2.1; it's a retrofit of some
of
  the most important bug fixes in V3.0 to V2.0, but they're all in V3.0.

  The upgrade process from 1.3 to 2.0 is about the same as 2.0 to 3.0, so
you
  can probably get by with your V3.0 directions -- but that's not a
guarantee.

  With a 2-step upgrade, I'd probably skip it and go straight to the
reinstall,
  but it will depend on how heavily customized your setup is and how
isolated
  your user data is from the OS data (i.e., separate partition or disk).
  In my case, I've got my user data pretty isolated (but I used update
  install for V2.0).

  Another complicating factor you don't mention is whether you have any
layered
  or third-party products installed; the more of these, the stronger the 
  argument for a full install becomes (but also the more caution needed;
many
  products have been slow in upgrading to V3.0).

[end of summary]
