>I am getting. Please help if you know anything I am doing wrong.
>
>The drive is on SCSI port 4, so I am first doing a
>
>disklabel -rw rz4 rz28

This is OK.

>Then I am stepping through (from a to h) and doing a
>
>newfs rrz4k rz28  (obviously with the k substituted with a through h)

This is NOT OK.  Default partition tables on drives in the disktab
almost all have some overlapping partitions.  You are not intended to
newfs every partition on the disk -- only ones that do not overlap.
For example, if you just wanted one big partition, you could just
newfs /dev/rrz4c.  Alternately the "a" and "g" partitions frequently
represent the DEC default operating system installation format --
a sizeable root partition, and a gigantic /usr partition.

So let's say you format "a" and "b" which generally don't overlap since
they are root and swap partitions in most cases.  Then you go and format
"c" which is the entire disk.  This clobbers "a" and "b".  Then you format
the next partition which is something less than the whole disk, which blows
away a chunk of the "c" partition you just formatted.  Are you getting
the picture?

>After this, a disklabel -r rz4 command shows all partitions with file systems
>and looking fine. 

Yes, your partition table is not damaged by this process.

>BTW, As a government DEC beta site who just bought about 300k worth of DEC
>equipment, I have been treated very badly by their help people. I have put in
>calls for the past two days and have received no responses at all. Their
>support is poor at best. (along with their documentation).

That's OK.  99% of unix vendors don't give support as well as usenet does
anyway.

