In article <bamon-280994115609@amon.cc.oberlin.edu>, bamon@ocvaxc.cc.oberlin.edu (Jennifer R. Amon) writes:
|>
|>Hi,
|>
|>I've got an axp 2000-300 running OSF1 v1.3. By the time we got ourselves
|>onto software maintenance, the 2.0 update had already been circulated, so
|>the first set of upgrade stuff I got was v2.1. I have no instructions for
|>the 2.0 upgrade, but I can get my hands on the CDs, because a set came with
|>another axp machine that was ordered later than mine. Now I also have the
|>3.0 upgrade info.
|>
|>Question: What are the pros and cons of going through the upgrade processes
|>(1.3 -> 2.0 -> 2.1 -> 3.0) vs. a new installation of v3.0. I don't have too
|>much software on this machine, but I'm also not too comfortable yet with
|>the
|>installation process in general, and the mail setup in particular.
|>
|>Thanks in advance,
|>   Jennifer
|>

If you choose to update, you can go from 1.3 -> 2.0 -> 3.0.  You don't need to 
do an update to 2.1 prior to jumping to 3.0.

If you've made any investment in customizing/configuring your system, then it
probably makes sense to do the updates since you won't have to repeat them.  This is especially true if you are concerned about setting up things like mail
again.

If your setup is simple and customizations are few, it might just be easier to
do a full installation to 3.0 and repeat the setups.  This allows you to 
change partitioning or system disks, if desired, and even to use the AdvFS
file system for root, usr, and/or var if you like.

Either way, please read the appropriate version of the Installation Guide and
Release Notes for whichever version(s) you decide to install.

Dave


