In article <36eggp$g5c@larch.cc.swarthmore.edu> speno@cc.swarthmore.edu (John P. Speno) writes:


 > Say I have this kernel config on a DEC 3000/400s running osf/1 1.3:

 > controller asc0 at tcds0 slot 0 vector ascintr 
 > device disk rz3 at asc0 drive 24 
 > controller asc1 at tcds0 slot 1 vector ascintr 
 > device disk rz8 at asc1 drive 64

 > The rz3 is an internal RZ26, rz8 is an external RZ57. Is there a
 > way to put the external drive on the same controller as the
 > internal (asc0) just by changing this config file?

It is my understanding that that won't work.  One of the beefs that I
have with my 3000/400 and 3000/600 models is no external scsi
connector to the internal scsi bus, like our DecStation 5000/240 has.
This severely limits the total number of devices that can be hooked up
to the internal scsi bus.

 > Aside: What is the relationship between the numbers (rz?) and the
 > drvies. I have another external drive (rz9) in another expansion
 > box, but how can I tell what drive is 8 and which is 9? There are
 > no scsi selector switches on the boxes the drives are in.  -- John
 > P. Speno, speno@cc.swarthmore.edu, Swarthmore College Computing
 > Center "There is no wisdom greater than kindness."

If the drives are different types, go into console mode and "show
device".  This will show what type of drive is at which scsi id.  The
scsi B bus is the external one.  Disk rz8 will appear as B/0 and rz9
is B/1.


