Mime-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=US-ASCII
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 7bit

    Well, I probably shouldn't follow up to my own post, but it's been a
while, and there are lots of otherstories out there now.

    Trying the update method on a 3000/300X running 2.1, I got a warning
that the root file system would be 108% full during part of the procedure,
but that that was probably OK. Checking the release notes and installation
instructions gave me an idea of roughly how much space I would need, and
that looked OK, too, so I proceeded with the upgrade.

   Big mistake. The whole thing crapped out with a filesystem full error,
leaving me with a trashed system, and worse yet, the tape dump I'd done
just before starting the upgrade had a hard error. (OK, so I should have
checked my biorhythms chart before trying this, right?) I managed to
restore enough of / and /usr from a dump done when we'd upgraded from 2.0
to 2.1 to tar the /usr/users tree and ftp it to another system, then did a
clean install (shades of unix as it used to be....): no problems.

    We have upgraded one other 3000/300X so far, and had no problems using
the upgrade method. That still leaves two more 3000/300X's, a 3000/400,
and a 3000/600.

    I should qualify my "no problems" statement by saying that, though
I've pondered the LAT documentation for 3.0 for some time, and edited the
new site-specific LAT file, while the service is seen by local terminal
servers, any attempt to log on to the system via LAT are refused. Anyone
seen this behavior/have a fix?

    Thanks,

                  Joe Gurman

