Problems: Too much swapping space allocated.  The program is
	  memory intensive. Since UNIX (or just OSF?) does not
	  immediately return space freed by "free" to system
	  (it claims new swapping space, not reuses those
	  freed space),  I have to maintain a pool of free nodes.
	  My program will first examine if the free pool is empty,
	  and will only do "malloc" when the pool is empty.

	  The main body of my program is a loop. It may loop
	  from 1 to 100 times, depending upon some command-line
	  parameter.
	
          The problem is that, every iteration of that loop
	  demands more swapping space.  This happens even if
	  the program does not do any new "malloc".  Each iteration
	  requires new swapping space, pure and simple.  As
	  a result, for some input, my program eventually exhausts
	  the entire 128M swapping space.

          Any way getting around this problem?

Thanks.


--

