In news.software.b, article <34q11e$4v4@mustang.mst6.lanl.gov>,
  carter@corvette.mst6.lanl.gov (Dave Carter) writes:
> i compiled INN with the security patch on an alpha running osf/1.
> everything's been going fine, but lately, i've noticed that the innd
> process has been growing.  it starts out at around 18M, and seems to
> grow slightly every day.  (it's currently up to 35M).  what is going
> on?
> 
Are you using the malloc that comes with INN?

Most malloc libraries try to be clever about coalescing free blocks and
searching for "best-fit" free blocks somehow. Schemes like that are known
to be broken in the sense that there's always a memory allocation pattern
which can fragment a memory pool (which means that the pool will grow
without bounds). The malloc coming with INN has no such problem, it's far
too stupid for that. Note that in this case, "stupid" also means "optimal
when used in a virtual-memory environment".

Some people even put that malloc in their system C library. ;-)

