: process.c:1364: too many arguments to function `setpgrp'
: *** Exit 1
: Stop.
: *** Exit 1
: Stop.

: Any clues?

Apply the following patch to fix the problem:

*** process.c.orig      Tue Sep 20 16:42:38 1994
--- process.c   Tue Sep 20 16:43:15 1994
***************
*** 1361,1367 ****
--- 1361,1371 ----
            /* In order to get a controlling terminal on some versions
               of BSD, it is necessary to put the process in pgrp 0
               before it opens the terminal.  */
+ #if defined (__osf__) && (defined (__alpha) || defined (__alpha__))
+           setpgid (0, 0);
+ #else
            setpgrp (0, 0);
+ #endif
  #endif
          }
  #endif /* TIOCNOTTY */

This fixed it for me. If you look at the man page for setpgid/setpgrp,
setpgrp is being phased out. Under OSF1 V2.0, the man page says:

   The setpgrp() function is supported by OSF/1 for binary compatibility only.

What the man page doesn't say is that the setpgrp() procedure takes no 
arguments (a la System V). The section of code where this call is assumes 
that one is on a BSD-like system where setpgrp() takes two arguments.
Hence the need to change the call from setpgrp to setpgid.

