Recently I've started experiencing problems with lockd on our mailserver
machine. This didn't happen under OSF 1.3. When I boot I see the message:

	rpc.lockd: Cannot contact status monitor

There are also messages in /var/adm/syslog.dated that say

	Sep 26 10:50:48 nobozo binmail[13017]: cannot lockf /usr/spool/mail/forrest

Also, users are reporting that the 'inc' command hangs after the
mailserver machine is rebooted. (This is because of the "cannot lockf"
message above). Is this supposed to happen?

The lockd man page isn't really clear. It says

"The rpc.lockd daemon then requests the status monitor daemon,
 rpc.statd, for monitor service. The reply to the lock request is
 not sent to the kernel until the status daemon and the server site's
 lock daemon have replied."

This says to me that lockd talks to statd and to another lockd. Since
the mailbox files are on the same machine as binmail these two
instances of lockd must be on the same machine, right? What would
cause lockd to not be able to contact statd on the same machine?
Or, is the message above refering to something else completely?

Further, it says

  If either the status monitor or server site's lock daemon is unavailable,
  the reply to a lock request for remote data is delayed until all daemons
  become available.

Running ps shows:

ps aux | grep rpc
forrest   1298  0.0  0.2 1.30M 288K p1  S  + 12:34:41     0:00.02 grep rpc
root       223  0.0  0.4 1.74M 608K ??  I    10:56:35     0:00.18 /usr/sbin/rpc.lockd
root       221  0.0  0.2 1.44M 328K ??  S    10:56:35     0:00.12 /usr/sbin/rpc.statd


so at least these processes are running.

