>   better if weren't held out as a panic-comfort zone in a 1.05Gb drive.

I've run with minfree set to 5% and I've noticed that the machine really
starts to drag when the spool is close to full.  I'd believe the quote
about the throughput degradation.

>   Anyone care to make recommendations?  I am considering dropping minfree 
>   to 2 or 3% and setting the optimization preference to time (vs. the man
>   recommended 'space') as fragmentation shouldn't be a serious problem on
>   a drive dedicated to USENET news articles.

At one point I was really my news spool was really hurting for space, so
I dropped minfree to 0%, and I had the news software set to shut down at
5 MB free on a 300 MB partition.  Things blew up when I ran out of free
blocks.  I still had more that 5 MB free according to df (about 6.5 MB as
I recall), but it was all frags, so files bigger than a K or so couldn't
be created.  Running clear full with minfree at 2 or 3% puts you in
the danger zone.

One thing that you may be able to do that is a real winner is to newfs
the spool partition with 4K blocks and 512 byte frags instead of 8K/1K.
This haves the amount of space wasted at the end of each file.  If you
do this, it is best to have all the big files, like history and the
logs on another partition, otherwise accesses to these files will slow
down.  Unfortunately, this is not adjustable by tunefs.  The filesystem
has to be set up this way with newfs.
