>   that I don't want to do this lightly.  Still, 100Mb could be used much 
>   better if weren't held out as a panic-comfort zone in a 1.05Gb drive.

We run the (nonstandard) 'dexpire' package to handle expires.  It
expires things based on relative importance of the groups to you,
rather than absolute 'time the article has been here', and it expires
however much stuff is necessary to leave a specified amount of free
space on the disk.

With that bit of technology as background, I set up dexpire to try to
leave 100 Meg free (on a 1 Gig disk), and run it every 3 hours to keep
things that way.  (It's fairly fast, since it doesn't update history.
We run normal expire with LOONG expire times once a day to catch that
up.  We also disable dexpire, and use only expire, on some selected
groups like *.answers and news.newusers, since dexpire also doesn't
honor Expires headers - which probably also speeds it up.)

Since that's 10%, I tunefs'ed that filesystem, which is used ONLY by
news, to have a minfree of 0%.  Works fine.  We get (at least) 900 Meg
of articles, plus whatever percent of the notionally- free 100Meg has
been taken up by new articles arriving since the last dexpire run.

Prior to that, we were running about 750 Meg articles, 150 Meg 'useable
free space' (freed by expire - of course, highly variable depending on
what had been happening recently), and 100 Meg 'lost' (reserved)
space.  Now, I've still got my 'performance' 10% free, but it's free in
a useful manner.  

