In article <35a38j$rol@ftp.UU.NET> chris@suite.sw.oz.au (Chris Maltby) writes:
>In <3584uk$mkm@ftp.UU.NET> rob@agate.berkeley.edu (Rob Robertson) writes:
>>it only changes it from time to space if you cross into your minfree
>>space.
>
>Actually, it changes when the number of free fragments gets to be too large
>a proportion of your free space.

Indeed - I also run at 5% minfree (on SunOS 4.1.3_U1), with most of the
95% used up (i.e. df typically reports 90-96%), and my spool changes
from TIME to SPACE and back just about every day. The paradoxical thing
is that it switches to SPACE when expire (fastrm actually) has run for a
while, and back to TIME during the day when it starts to fill up again -
i.e. exactly the opposite of what one would naively expect.

The reason is, of course, that the expiration increases fragmentation
drastically by causing lots of previously full blocks to be only
partially filled, while running with SPACE optim for a while thereafter
reduces fragmentation to an acceptable level again by filling up the
blocks.

I don't believe you ever get these spontaneous TIME/SPACE changes unless
you have minfree below 10%, though.

