In article <34l0b5$nrd@canopus.cc.umanitoba.ca>,
Andrew Drobot <adrobot@access.mbnet.mb.ca> wrote:
> login: TEST
> \PASSWORD:   
...

This is the "teletype hack". To make UNIX work on uppercase-only teletype
model ASR33 terminals, if you logged in all uppercase it set a mode in the
terminal driver called "lcase". In lcase mode, uppercase on input is mapped
to lowercase, lowercase on output is mapped to uppercase. The backslashes
mark real uppercase characters.

To turn this off, do "stty -lcase".

I'd recommend putting "stty -lcase" in your global login/profile files, unless
you have an asr33 teletype somewhere. Or get the source to "login" and take
the teletype hack out (see gatekeeper.dec.com, there's FreeBSD and NetBSD
both of which have the source to the whole login/passwd/... suite of programs
in their source trees).

I'm kinda surprised DEC still has the teletype hack enabled.
