In article <Cvv91u.3DE@ideta.nl> hildo@ideta.nl (Hildo den Breejen) writes:
> I am into the UID business myself these days, so I read Posix and Xopen
> on this subject.
> 
> Both use the phrase 'appropriate privilege', where I would say 'root'.
> Their must be some subtle difference. Perhaps someone can explain.

This is because POSIX is allowing for the possibility of so-called
secure configurations.  Traditional Unix-like security has various
holes and so more secure configurations with names like C2 and B1 have
been defined in standards documents.  These configurations allow for
more gradations in privilege than "root" and "non-root".  What this
adds up to is that on a normal configuration, "appropriate privilege"
and "root" are synonymous.  When configured for security, however,
there may be a distinction.
