>campus ethernet is not subnetted, but it is bridged.  I can't rule out
>heavy network traffic yet, but I don't think it likely as I'm not seeing
>unusual delays in other network applications.

I got one helpful reply from someone at DEC, which pointed out some of the
things to look for.  After poking around a bit with various network monitoring
tools, we are still somewhat mystified.  We determined that there was a
significant noise source on one part of the network.  When that noise
source was dumping packets on the same segment as the server and
client, NSF basically ground to a halt.  The noise source was putting
out lots of packets with framing and CRC errors, and some short packets
(according to etherpeek), to the tune of about 5% total.  Putting the
noise source back on the other side of the bridge (we hadn't identified
its exact location at that point) freed up the NFS to complete its
transactions.  We have now eliminated the noise source.  Between the
time we put the bridge back in between and the time the source went
away I did not see a repeat of the earlier (less disasterous) NFS
delays, nor have I seen any since.  Perhaps the problem is fixed, but
if so, why?  I will keep monitoring.

My biggest question is, why would this noise source affect NFS so strongly,
yet leave, for example, telnet terminal sessions (across the same segment
the NFS traffic was traversing) performing apparently normally?

Also, I have now also played around with the NFS settings, and am
wondering what other people have found to be optimal for the following
situation:  basically we have two machines that are pretty much clones
of each other, with different client bases: we've split the faculty/staff
onto one machine and the students onto another.  The faculty machine is
less heavily loaded, and is therefore the server for most things (which
also makes it slightly more reliable from the users point of veiw <wry grin>)
including NFS serving a common partition on which I have installed software
and so on: /usr/local.  I've shared it R/W for ease of administration.
I'm also sharing another partition: the news spool.

So, given that updates to the /usr/local partition are infrequent and
made only by me and certain system deamons, what parameters would
people suggest using?  I'm inclined to greatly increase rsize and to
considerably lengthen actimeo.  What would be appropriate values?

--David


