Name         Type      ext/in Ext+I/D       Int     FP   Date  Obtained
------------ --------- ------ ------------ ------ ------ ----- -----------------

Sun SC1102   2xSP/Sup  50     1M+20/16      2730   3681  May93 c.s.sun.hw
DEC 2100     A21064    ??/190 1M+16/16      2994   3841
Sun SC1104   4xSP/Sup  50     1M+20/16      5318   7076  May93 c.s.sun.hw
DEC 2100     2xA21064  ??/190 1M+16/16      5778   7279
Sun SC2108   8xSP/Sup  40     1M+20/16      8047  10600  May93 c.s.sun.hw
Sun SC1108   8xSP/Sup  50     1M+20/16     10113  12710  May93 c.s.sun.hw
DEC 2100     4xA21064  ??/190 1M+16/16     11113  13025
Sun SC2216   16xSP/Sup 50     2M+20/16     21196  28064  Oct93 sunflash
DEC 7760     6xA21064A ??/275 4M+16/16     24735  40103  Sep94 DEC

I've sorted things by SPECrateint performance, so in absolute performance,
the OSF/1 implementation looks ok with our hardware. It should be noted
that SUN needs 2-3 processors to match a single Alpha processor, so I've
grouped it slightly differently for scaling with additional processors:

2100 A500MP:    SPECrateint             SPECratefp
1 CPU           1                       1
2 CPU           1.92                    1.89
4 CPU           3.71                    3.39

7700:           SPECrateint             SPECratefp

1 CPU           1                       1
2 CPU           1.90                    2.0
4 CPU           3.86                    4.04
6 CPU           5.47                    6.00

SC 1000:        SPECrateint             SPECratefp
2 CPU           1                       1
4 CPU           1.94                    1.92
8 CPU           3.70                    3.45

This shows that OSF/1 makes as good or better use of additional processors
as the version of Solaris they benchmarked the SparcCenter 1000 with. The
better than linear scaling for the 7700 is due to cacheing effects, where
