ccvj@misty.latrobe.edu.au (Vicki Jordan) writes:

>Could someone please tell me how to change a process's uid the right way?
>I need to start as root, change to the uid of the person allocated the
>process and then change back to root.  This contrived code illustrates
>the problem and the output my confusion!  Running suid root, I get
>different behaviour across Ultrix, AIX and OSF1.  It would seem to me
>that AIX and OSF1 are "correct" when run as an individual user, but only
>OSF1 correct when run as root.  In partilcuar, I need to get the same
>result from AIX as OSF1 in both the run as user and root cases.

>Is there anyone who doesn't confuse as easily as I do??  If so, please
>enlighten me!


>Thanks.
>--------

>#include <stdio.h>
>#include <sys/types.h>

>main()
>{

>    printf("Start             - uid is %d and euid is %d \n", getuid(), geteuid());

>/* Change to uid 203 */
>    seteuid(203);
>    printf("Call seteuid(203) - uid is %d and euid is %d \n", getuid(),geteuid());
>    setuid(203);
>    printf("Call setuid(203)  - uid is %d and euid is %d \n", getuid(),geteuid());

>/* Change back to initial uid 0 */
>    seteuid(0);
>    printf("Call seteuid(0)   - uid is %d and euid is %d \n", getuid(),geteuid());
>    setuid(0);
>    printf("Call setuid(0)    - uid is %d and euid is %d \n", getuid(),geteuid());
>}

>File is suid root.

>Ultrix - run as user 203:
>Start             - uid is 203 and euid is 0 
>Call seteuid(203) - uid is 203 and euid is 203 
>Call setuid(203)  - uid is 203 and euid is 203 
>Call seteuid(0)   - uid is 203 and euid is 203 

	Obviously, ultrix does not have saved-set-UID (Posix optional)
	there's no way you cab regain root privileges.

>Call setuid(0)    - uid is 203 and euid is 203 

	Nope. You don't get root back.

>Aix - run as user 203:
>Start             - uid is 203 and euid is 0 
>Call seteuid(203) - uid is 203 and euid is 203 
>Call setuid(203)  - uid is 203 and euid is 203 
>Call seteuid(0)   - uid is 203 and euid is 0 

	So AIX does have saved-set-UID. You can retrieve your root privs.

>Call setuid(0)    - uid is 0 and euid is 0 

	Check. You're effectively root now. root can do anything.


>OSF1 - run as user 203
>Start             - uid is 203 and euid is 0 
>Call seteuid(203) - uid is 203 and euid is 203 
>Call setuid(203)  - uid is 203 and euid is 203 
>Call seteuid(0)   - uid is 203 and euid is 0 
>Call setuid(0)    - uid is 0 and euid is 0 

	Obviously, OSF has saved-set-UID too.

>Ultrix/AIX - run as root:
>Start             - uid is 0 and euid is 0 
>Call seteuid(203) - uid is 0 and euid is 0 
>Call setuid(203)  - uid is 203 and euid is 203 
>Call seteuid(0)   - uid is 203 and euid is 203 
>Call setuid(0)    - uid is 203 and euid is 203 

	AIX obviously does not have'em.

>OSF1 - run as root:
>Start             - uid is 0 and euid is 0 
>Call seteuid(203) - uid is 0 and euid is 203 
>Call setuid(203)  - uid is 0 and euid is 203 
>Call seteuid(0)   - uid is 0 and euid is 0 
>Call setuid(0)    - uid is 0 and euid is 0 

	Apparently, OSF1 has saved-set-UID's, but considders root
	not to have 'appropriate privileges': a setuid root process can only
	do what any user can:
	make euid equal to uid or make euid equal to saved-set-UID.
	It cannot change it's real UID. (It can in BSD, using setreuid()).

