|>
|>I am using the C++ compiler of DEC cxx with OSF 2.0, 
|>           DEC C++ V1.3A-0 for DEC OSF/1 (Alpha)
|>and I try to declare and use
|>an array of class object (with no default constructor). I have some 
|>problem (i.e. a Segmentation fault) and I want to know if it is because
|>I am doing wrong or because of a compiler bug
|>Here is the example:
|>
|>#include <stdio.h>
|>#include <iostream.h>
|>class A {
|>private:
|>  int N;
|>  float *tab;
|>public:
|>  A(int n):N(n) { tab=new float[N]; }
|>  ~A() { delete tab; }
|>  float & operator()(int i) { return tab[i]; } 
|>  friend ostream& operator<<(ostream &s,A &X);
|>};
	.
	.
	.
|>				Marc
|>
|>PS: its a compiler BUG, because the program work on a SUN with cfront v2.0

Marc,
   This is definitely not a compiler bug.  Your program has several common
memory allocation and deallocation problems in it.  The reason it works on
another system with another compiler is sheer accident.

   In particular, you're implicitly copying objects with a bitwise copy
of the class.  This means that you end up with more than one copy of the
pointer to the array of floats.  That's OK the *first* time you call delete
(for one copy), but it's definitely a bad idea for any succeeding calls
(like deleting a copy).

   Get a copy of Scott Meyer's excellent "Effective C++; 50 Specific Ways
to Improve Your Programs and Designs" (Addison Wesley Publishers, ISBN
0-201-56534-9) and look at #11.

   DECladebug makes it easy to see what's going on:

