In article <34pedbINNbbt@usenet.pa.dec.com>, neideck@nestvx.enet.dec.com 
(Burkhard Neidecker-Lutz) writes:

>We couldn't handle SMP at all under OSF/1 until recently, so there was
>some credibility to these claims in the past. Of course, this isn't true
>anymore since OSF/1 V3.0 is out. The compilers that do
>automatic decomposition have been out for some time on VMS, so they are
>well tested (it's the same GEM compilers on all the platforms).

In the interest of accuracy, I have to point out that for those languages
which support decomposition, it isn't GEM that's doing it.  For
Fortran-77 and C, parallel decomposition is handled by the optional
KAP preprocessor.  For Fortran 90, it's done in a separate transformation
stage of the compiler and is more directed at workstation farms than
SMP.

However, it is true that the compilers are GEM-based and are substantially
similar to those on OpenVMS.  In addition, recent releases of GEM-based
compilers have new optimizations which can produce significant
performance advantages.  There's more coming, including profile-directed
optimizations.

