I don't think the memory consumption is plainly x 2 ( from experience in
a lot of benchmarks and porting efforts), but supposing at runtime
an application uses large in-memory linked lists or pointers to objects
of whatever size, remember then that you are on a 64 bit machine where
pointer addresses consume more then 32 bits.

Anyhow, this is the same discussion as in the 16 to 32 bit migration
time. There is no such thing as a free lunch in my opinion. Today very few
people make a problem of a 32 bit system consuming more then a 16 bit
system. In a few years when other vendors *will* introduce 64 bit OS's, also this
discussion will fade away. And frankly spoken, Digital will have the advantage
of having a lot of experience optimizing memory use for 64 bit ;-)


