In article <CvK9EG.9pF@news.cern.ch>, Dan Pop <danpop@cernapo.cern.ch> wrote:
> The OS is a memory pig:

Yes, indeed !

> So, a plain egrep takes 1.3 MB of virtual memory and the X server 37.6
> MB! Until a couple of weeks ago I was running the same X clients on a
> (non-DEC) X terminal with only 8 MB of RAM.

On my system, the X server uses 10.6 virtual Megabytes (I've been
logged in for two weeks with a reasonable amount of windows open in
the four workspaces I use (thanks ctwm)). 32 is probably more than
normal (maybe sign of a bug (like the -terminate "bug" with xdm), or
maybe because the size has grown that big at some point... don't
forget the virtual size almost never "grows back" (mfree doesn't help
here)).

Also keep in mind that the Mach microkernel uses VM tricks all the
time (like copy-on-write), which implies that the virtual size
reported by ps is not always correct (the kernel is reported as 280M,
even though only 100M is reserved on the swap (70M actually used)).

On the other hand the RSS size (this one seems more or less correct,
if you compare with the vmstat numbers) is more interesting since it
correspond to real memory use.


	Stefan


