In article <34l774$kq7@riscsm.scripps.edu>,
Matt Harrington <matt@perutz.salk.edu> wrote:
>I installed 64 MB RAM into an Alpha 3000/800.  The firmware tests
>for memory passed.  I rebuilt the kernel and rebooted.  My question
>is, how can I tell if the new kernel recognizes and uses the new 
>memory?  Something like the SGI IRIX command 'hinv' would be useful.
>
>Matt Harrington
>

The closest thing to IRIX's "hinv" would be a command such as
this: uerf -R | more
When the system boots it tells you how much memory it sees.
In DEC OSF/1 versions before 3.0 it always reports 2MB less than your
physical memory, with 3.0 it reports the exact amount. It's
these messages that get recorded in the uerf log file which you
see with the above command.

You shouldn't need to rebuild the kernel when adding/removing
memory.
