Three cents more about nu/TPU from a/Soft.  We've moved a lot of our
long-term VMS usage to OSF/1 in the past year.  For many of us (computer
staff) and our users that means finding a satisfactory editor.  For
casual e-mail editing we are promoting Pine/Pico and that works well
for that purpose.  I've taken a careful look at Emacs and Vi and have
decided that for all their power and ubiquity that I'm not willing to
expend the level of energy it would take to become really proficient
with them.  And neither are a number of other staff members.  And even
less inclined to do so is our user community that we migrated.  So the 
search for another editor.  I've seen comments off and on about bugs etc. 
in nu/TPU so it wasn't until exhausting other possibilities that I 
contacted the company.

First, they can set you up with a demo to try.  All the capabilities
except you can't save more than 100 lines.  And they offered us as
a University a reasonable (though not cheap) licensing deal.  Second,
I think you have to take into account what you are used to doing with
TPU/EVE.  We have had thousands of users using it on our VMS systems
for years, but from reading others' comments we are obviously not
power users in our expectations.  E.g., as far as I know no one here ever 
wrote their own TPU code.  Third, in our tryout of nu/TPU,
which was limited and not exhaustive, the only thing we immediately
turned up was the inability to do a hanging indent (negative margin
shift for first line of a paragraph).  Phone support seemed pretty
good -- no problem getting right through to someone and, in the case
of the hanging indent, at least I got immediate confirmation that it
did not work, was just not implemented (what would happen in an analogous
case calling DEC support?).

Of course, some of my colleagues are aghast at the idea of pursuing
the holy grail of TPU/EVE to run on an OSF/1 system!  But they still
talk to me, so ...   

