Adding a LAT Printer to an OSF/1 System
---------------------------------------

Source: Mark G. Thorne, instructor of DEC OSF/1 System Administration Course.

1. Find and reserve a LAT terminal name to use for the printer.

   - Look in /etc/inittab at the last line.  It will appear approximately
     as follows:

     lat0z:3:respawn:/usr/sbin/getty /dev/tty0z console vt100

   - From this you can determine that the name to use is '/dev/tty0z'.

   - You must change one field in the line so that it look like this:

     lat0z:3:off:/usr/sbin/getty     /dev/tty0z console vt100
             ^^^

   - As root, execute...

     init q

     ...to have init re-read /etc/inittab.


2. Manually assign the LAT terminal name to a specific port on a particular
   LAT terminal server.

   - Once the port and server names have been determeined issue the
     following command:

     latcp -A -p tty0z -H SERVER_NAME -R PORT_NAME

     ...or...

     latcp -A -p tty0z -H SERVER_NAME -V SERVICE_NAME

     where: tty0z is the terminal found in (1), above, and SERVER_NAME,
            PORT_NAME, and SERVICE_NAME are for your terminal server that
            serves this printer.  NOTE: These MUST be given in UPPER-CASE.

     NOTE: The port on the terminal server must be set to 'access remote'
           for any of this to work.

     - You should now be able to copy a file to the target printer
       manually:

     cp $HOME/.profile /dev/tty0z


3. Establish the terminal and printer information for the Line Printer
   Daemon (lpd):

   - Invoke lprsetup and specify the following fields:

   ct  LAT             # must be UPPER-CASE
   ts  SERVER_NAME     # must be UPPER-CASE
   lp  /dev/tty0z                          
   op  PORT_NAME       # must be UPPER-CASE
             ...or...                      
   os  SERVICE_NAME    # must be UPPER-CASE


4. Test the printer by issuing lpr commands.

5. Establish the terminal/server/port/service correspondance permanently.

   - Modify the /sbin/init.d/lat by locating the block of code shown below
     and modify it by adding the same latcp issued manually before:

	if [ "$9" = "S" ]
	then	if [ "$8" = "0" ]
		then	# This code is only executed the *first* time the
                        # system enters run level 3.  All latcp commands used
			# for customizing LAT (such as adding additional
			# services, changing group codes, changing the multi-
			# cast timer, etc.) should follow the 'latcp -r'
			# command below.  As an example, to define a service
			# name other than the node name and add group codes
			# for this service, use the following commands:
			# /usr/sbin/latcp -A -a new_service -i "DEC OSF/1"
			# /usr/sbin/latcp -g52 -a new_service
			# The 'latcp -r' command below will add a default
			# service (and have the same name as the LAT node name,
			# which will default to the system name).  If this
			# service name is not desired, comment out the
			# 'latcp -r' command and replace it with the
			# appropriate 'latcp -A -a' command.
			/usr/sbin/latcp -r
                        /usr/sbin/latcp -A -p tty0z -H SERVER_NAME -R PORT_NAME

                        ...or...

                        /usr/sbin/latcp -A -p tty0z -H SERVER_NAME -V SERVICE_NAME
		fi



