In article <ken.cunefare.1129254349B@news.gatech.edu>,
Kenneth A. Cunefare <ken.cunefare@me.gatech.edu> wrote:
>On several occasions recently, I have received the message "no more
>processes" after attempting to issue a command. The system's process limit
>is currently set at a rather high number. The problem does not seem to
>depend to much on the amount of user activity. The problem has occured with
>as few as 3 users logged in, and I was 2 of them. There does not appear to
>be an inordinate number of processes running (ps -e).
>
>Any ideas? Causes? Cures? Please e-mail to address below. Thanks

You're probably running up against the per-user max process limit (maxuprc,
defaults to 64).  You can get around it by changing your kernel config file
to have a line like this:

maxuprc   128

which will give you 128 processes per user.  You may want to tweak maxusers
up some if you set maxuprc high so that you don't run out of process slots. 


