Ah, reality strikes.  The disk is thumping, so indeed you are doing
I/O, regardless of what ps says.  It might be VM activity or it might
be file system activity.  DEC OSF/1 has a unified buffer cache so free
pages can be used by either the file system or the VM (for text/data).
But, you need to characterize your workload if we are to help you tune
your system to try to avoid this kind of bad response time.

Without more info, I suggest you read the OSF/1 Performance Tuning
Guide a bit to see if you can tune the system some.  In particular, you
might want to tune 'ubcmaxpercent' so that the file system doesn't eat
all the free pages which in turn lets the VM gets a better chance at
keeping text/data resident.

But, if memory is overcommitted tuning isn't much help.  Your vmstat
below shows that you have 2+74+35 = 111 threads running on the system.
That's 32 MB divided 111 ways or 300 kb per thread, more or less
(massive hand wave here, but it'll do for now).  What are all those
threads doing?  Each one has unavoidable fixed memory costs associated
with them.

