>have 196 MB RAM and the exp dump file size is about 500MB.
>
>We have a performance problem: when we have many large batch reports
>running the interactive response time is slow. And when I look at
>"vmstat" output, the run queue is about 15. I think that the problem is
>that the big reports are very CPU intensive (after having loaded all
>their data into Oracle databases buffers in RAM) and that they use all
>of their CPU timeslice. So the interactive processes have to wait long
>before they get their slice of the CPU - i.e. the run queue is long.

Sounds reasonable. I assume you've tried using 'renice' to push
down the scheduling priority of the big jobs.  

FYI, I have an alias that expands to:

ps axo pid,pri,cp,pcpu,pmem,usertime,systime,sl,nvcsw,nivcsw,ucomm

which prints many things, among them IVCSW, the number of involuntary
context switches.  This should show you which procs run out of time and
get preempted.

>How do I reduce the maximum timeslice a CPU-intensive process can get in
>OSF/1? I read man an hour yesterday without getting any info. They
>referred to a printed manual which I don't have at hand.

You can't with the standard kernel - it's hardcoded.  If you build a
real-time kernel, I believe each process can call posix style library
functions to ask for custom schedule policies.  I think you can then
write small wrapper programs that set the policies you want then exec
your apps.  But please don't go on this - get out the real-time manual
and make sure I'm remembering all of this correctly.  And the real-time
kernel has plusses and minuses: the extra functionality adds
path length to many things.

--
