(Here is the official statment about the status of OSF's OSF/1.)

The latest release of the OSF/1 Operating System, Release 1.3, was made available to 
licensees in June of 1994.  This was a full microkernel version of OSF/1 and is also 
conformant with the current draft of Specification 1170. New features added in OSF/1 
Release 1.3 include a Data Capture Interface (DCI), a Common Data Link Interface (CDLI) 
and support for 64-bit systems.

With this release, OSF/1 transitions to OSF's new business model, whereby future 
enhancements will be accomplished using the Pre-Structured Technology (PST) development 
process.  In the PST process, project sponsors take the initiative in proposing to OSF a 
complete development package, including both the requirements and a proposed solution. 
Work accomplished under the PST process requires approval from the OSF Board of 
Directors and funding from the project sponsors before proceeding.

In addition, the OSF Research Institute is continuing its advanced development work on 
the evolution of the microkernel to address massively parallel systems, cluster 
technology, real-time environments, trusted computing, and multiple operating system 
personalities.



