I have a problem with floating point on our OSF/1 machine (It's a DEC 
Alpha).  IEEE specifies that the result of dividing anything by 0
should be INF or -INF (depending on the numerator), with the exception
that 0/0 and INF/INF are both NaN (Not A Number).

On our alpha/OSF/1, These procuded FP Exceptions and exited with a
core dump.  I found the man page ieee(3), and it gave some suggestions,
none of which did what I wanted.

If you set it up to ignore SIGFPE, the result of 1/0 is 0 (not INF).
It suggested the compiler flag -resumption_safe, which it says will
make it produce the correct IEEE result, but it still gives 0.
I tried the flag on the cc compiler.  gcc doesn't even have the flag.

Is there any way to get this to behave correctly?
